WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.060
There's a lot to like in Ant-Man and the Wasp,
the newest offering from the Marvel Cinematic

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:08.069
Universe, but it's definitely not without
its flaws.

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:11.760
We're here to take a look at what worked…
and what might have worked a little better.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.209
These are the five best and five worst things
about Ant-Man and the Wasp.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:18.690
Best: Dad of the year

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:22.490
Some of the best moments in the film are when
Paul Rudd's Scott Lang is hanging out with

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.160
his young daughter Cassie, played by Abby
Ryder Fortson.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.550
The chemistry between Paul Rudd and Fortson
is exceptional, with Fortson bantering with

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.530
Rudd like a seasoned professional.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.760
It's hard to believe the two aren't actually
father and daughter in real life, and with

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.140
a relationship this strong to anchor the film,
everything else comes together beautifully.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:42.430
Worst: Previously on Ant-Man...

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:46.469
Ant-Man and the Wasp is a sequel to Ant-Man
as well as Captain America: Civil War, so

00:00:46.469 --> 00:00:49.070
there's a lot of story to fill viewers in
on.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:53.079
Unfortunately, the first several minutes of
Ant-Man and the Wasp are bogged down by clumsy

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:54.079
exposition.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:57.980
From Hank and Hope sitting down to remember
the plot of Ant-Man to Randall Park's Jimmy

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:02.649
Woo rehashing the plot of Civil War and Age
of Ultron, it's a lot of re-hashing from a

00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:06.030
writing team that is capable of more interesting
things.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.040
Best: Ghost isn't transparent

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.470
Hannah John-Kamen's Ghost doesn't even seem
to realize she's a villain, and that's because

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:15.990
her motivation is pretty simple: survive.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:19.689
Her quantum powers are tearing her body apart,
and she doesn't have long to live.

00:01:19.689 --> 00:01:23.590
In putting our heroes up against a villain
whose goal isn't world domination but simple

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:28.760
survival, the film feels fresh and distinct
from the 19 MCU outings that precede it.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:32.220
"Who is the bad guy in Ant-Man and the Wasp?

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:36.500
Mmmm, we have opponents but not necessarily
villains."

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.340
Worst: They can't all be winners

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:43.650
On the other hand, Walton Goggins appears
in the film as a shady black market tech dealer

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:47.299
named Sonny Burch, and the role is everything
Ghost isn't.

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:51.470
While Ghost has a compelling backstory and
a clearly defined personality, Burch is about

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:52.650
as bland as they come.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:56.590
The film is saved by Ghost's antagonistic
menace, but it's hard not to regret seeing

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:59.610
Walton Goggins wasted in a forgettable role.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:02.310
Best: Michael Peña steals the show

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:06.399
The best heist in the original Ant-Man isn't
Scott breaking into Hank's house, it's Michael

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:08.689
Pena's Luis stealing the show.

00:02:08.689 --> 00:02:10.050
He shines every time he's onscreen.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.050
"Got their credentials?"

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:12.050
"He's in the system."

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:13.050
"I'm in the system?"

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:14.050
"You're in the system."

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.430
"The system?"

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.980
Pena is once again a standout in a film packed
with memorable actors.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.020
His rapport with Michael Douglas's Hank Pym
is still delightfully awkward, and they even

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.510
manage to work in one of his sloppily narrated
stories.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.489
"I first met Scotty in Cell Block D and I
say 'I'm Luis,' and he says 'I'm scotty, and

00:02:30.489 --> 00:02:31.489
we're gonna be best friends.'

00:02:31.489 --> 00:02:32.489
Scotty gets out of jail and that's when he
met Hope and she's all 'look at my hairdo,

00:02:32.489 --> 00:02:33.489
I'm all business."

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:35.349
Wherever Ant-Man goes in the future, here's
hoping Luis goes with him.

00:02:35.349 --> 00:02:38.019
Worst: Can Janet catch a break?

00:02:38.019 --> 00:02:42.109
Janet's disappearance prior to the first film
pushes Hank into his angry, reclusive state

00:02:42.109 --> 00:02:44.639
and screws up his relationship with his daughter.

00:02:44.639 --> 00:02:48.450
Ant-Man and the Wasp has the opportunity to
undo this while giving one of the great Marvel

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.199
characters her MCU due.

00:02:50.199 --> 00:02:55.059
Instead, it just trades one trope for another,
making Janet one of the film's primary MacGuffins.

00:02:55.059 --> 00:02:59.650
When she finally appears in the end, she gets
maybe five minutes of screentime.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.099
Janet - and Michelle Pfeiffer - deserves better.

00:03:02.099 --> 00:03:04.579
Best: Low stakes, high reward

00:03:04.579 --> 00:03:09.559
Avengers: Infinity War took the MCU to the
highest stakes the franchise has ever seen,

00:03:09.559 --> 00:03:13.810
and came on the heels of an Asgardian apocalypse
and a Wakandan revolution.

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:17.370
Luckily, Ant-Man and the Wasp gives viewers
a bit of a break from the ridiculous tension

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:18.949
of the last MCU movie.

00:03:18.949 --> 00:03:22.339
The fate of the world isn't at stake in this
new Ant-Man adventure.

00:03:22.339 --> 00:03:26.719
At its core, Ant-Man and the Wasp is about
the fight to save the lives of two people,

00:03:26.719 --> 00:03:28.010
Ghost and Janet van Dyne.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:33.269
It's a comparatively small story, but just
what MCU fans needed after Infinity War.

00:03:33.269 --> 00:03:35.339
Worst: Time is money

00:03:35.339 --> 00:03:39.930
These days, most blockbusters have a run time
of over two hours, even though longer isn't

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.079
necessarily better.

00:03:41.079 --> 00:03:44.621
With comparatively small stakes and such a
simple story to tell, Ant-Man and the Wasp

00:03:44.621 --> 00:03:48.079
is absolutely a film that would benefit from
a shorter runtime.

00:03:48.079 --> 00:03:49.430
Even the director himself agrees.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:52.739
"I'm very vocal about the Ant-Man movies being
under 2 hours."

00:03:52.739 --> 00:03:57.449
Unfortunately, it comes in at over two hours,
longer than the first Ant-Man film, Doctor

00:03:57.449 --> 00:03:59.920
Strange, and even the first two Thor movies.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.579
You'd think a movie about shrinking heroes
would know that bigger isn't always better.

00:04:04.579 --> 00:04:06.659
Best: Laugh it up

00:04:06.659 --> 00:04:11.579
In the past, MCU movies have had a bad habit
of incessant quipping; even the previous Ant-Man

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.790
movie did it.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.420
"It's okay!"

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:24.100
As funny as Ant-Man and the Wasp is, there
isn't a single important dramatic moment in

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:28.210
the film that gets interrupted by a character
making a joke or a gag going down.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:32.240
Serious moments are given the time to be taken
seriously, and that makes the comedic moments

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:33.490
even better.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:35.770
Worst: Character matters

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:39.680
The MCU excels at a lot of things, but its
best quality might be its ability to take

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.909
characters and make them grow organically
from film to film.

00:04:42.909 --> 00:04:46.039
Unfortunately, this is Ant-Man and the Wasp's
biggest flaw.

00:04:46.039 --> 00:04:49.169
Scott might grow as a character, but it's
literal growth, not emotional.

00:04:49.169 --> 00:04:52.930
He ends the movie in the same spot he ended
in the first Ant-Man: romantically involved

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:56.030
with Hope, and no longer in immediate trouble
with the law.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:59.590
Meanwhile, Hope experiences a significant
life event in her mother returning from the

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:03.680
Quantum Realm, but the film never really shows
us what this means for her past the initial

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:04.680
reunion.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:08.460
Despite the size-changing, neither character
gets a chance to really change as people.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:09.460
Thanks for watching!

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:11.639
